The decay of heavy particles provides valuable information for studying particle dynamics and is also significant for searching for new particles beyond the standard model. To explore these areas, precise measurements of form factors in seminary weak decays are necessary. Recently, the Belle II experiment reported precise measurements of the form factor shape for the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay. Building on this, Belle II can target and measure the form factors for other meson decay transitions like $B^0ightarrow D^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ to expand the range of known form factor measurements. To facilitate these searches, theoretical calculations of these decay rates are necessary. While the Clebsch-Gordan identity suggests the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ and $B^0ightarrow D^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay rates have similar forms, precise measurements reveal slight differences. To resolve this, we calculate the differential decay rate differential with respect to the angle between the charged current and a particle and confirm that variations in precision lead to slight differences in the form factor shape.
